      "content": "What is a Competitor? Last Updated : 18 Dec, 2024 Comments Improve Suggest changes Like Article Like Report A competitor is a person or firm that is involved in competition with other people or organizations to attain certain goals or to be ahead of others. In business the competitors are industries, organizations, or companies that offer similar products and/or services to the same target market to sell the products to the customers and gain a larger market share. The other citizens who fall under this type of competitor are in sports, academics, and anything that involves rivalry and ranking. Competition is good since it helps organizations and businesses to improve in their endeavor to perform better than the next person, and gain fame and wealth. Table of Content Types of Competitors Benefits of Competitors Drawbacks of Competitors How to Analyze Competitor Performance? Competitor Strategies to Outperform Responding to Competitors Types of Competitors: 1. Direct Competitors: Supply equivalent goods or services to the targeted clients. Example: Coca-Cola vs. Pepsi. 2. Indirect Competitors: Supply items that cater to the same want or fulfill the same purpose as your competitor\u2019s products but under a different brand name. Example: The fast food segment with McDonald's as its type, will be compared to the casual dining segment which has Applebee\u2019s as its type. 3. Substitute Competitors: Suggest a similar product or service that may be used as a substitute for another. Example: Busses, subways, taxi, metro vs. online applications for car services such as Uber. 4. Potential Competitors: Firms that are outside the market but which have the potential and desire to move in.Firms in the product-market that can influence the market between now and some time in the future. Example: Apple the technology company diversifying into the electric car industry or division. 5. Future Competitors: It is very important for a company to identify those business entities, with which it might turn into a competitor based on the existing or newly emerged market tendencies or focus changes. Example: Beginning companies in a rapidly evolving field of technology. 6. Niche Competitors: Market niche, or a few choice, and usually, relatively slender sections of the total market with designated products or services. Example: A localized coffee selling shop versus a global brand coffee selling shop such as Starbucks. Benefits of Competitors: 1. Innovation Stimulation: It compels the companies to formulate strategies to differentiation themselves, thus designing new products or services. 2. Quality Improvement: Leads businesses to increase the quality of their products and services, in an attempt to satisfy clients. 3. Price Regulation: Prevents formation of monopolies, promotes setting of good prices by various companies that give the general consumer a wide range of acquireable prices. 4. Customer Focus: Encourages corporate entities to be more receptive of customers\u2019 requirements and demands. 5. Efficiency Enhancement: Right from enhancing organizational productivity to cutting back on organizational expenses, it encourages businesses. 6. Market Growth: Grows the overall market as businesses attempt to gain more clients. 7. Diverse Choices: Satisfies the consumer needs more by offering them a larger variety thus improving on consumer satisfaction. 8. Benchmarking Opportunities: Enables organizations to compare with other organizations in the sector to see which aspects of operations require attention. 9. ndustry Standards: Fosters: it plays a key role in the setting of healthy competition within the industry hence creating a competitive edge and meeting of the required industry standards. 10. Talent Attraction: Entice skilled workers to the industry since companies are in search of employees that will give them advantages. Drawbacks of Competitors: 1. Price Wars: Potential to cause price cuts to become unprofitable for som